WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.697
A Bash script should invoke the Google
Cloud CLI in a predictable and reviewable

2
00:00:07.777 --> 00:00:08.207
way.

3
00:00:08.287 --> 00:00:15.699
The safer pattern is a fixed command
structure with clearly separated options and

4
00:00:15.779 --> 00:00:16.746
operands.

5
00:00:16.826 --> 00:00:24.238
Avoid building one large command string
from variable data and asking the shell to

6
00:00:24.318 --> 00:00:26.145
interpret it again.

7
00:00:26.225 --> 00:00:32.133
That style makes quoting harder, hides
behavior from reviewers,

8
00:00:32.213 --> 00:00:36.617
and can behave unexpectedly when input
changes.

9
00:00:36.697 --> 00:00:43.572
Quote values that should remain literal
and use arrays where option lists or

10
00:00:43.652 --> 00:00:46.445
operands need to be assembled.

11
00:00:46.525 --> 00:00:53.078
Validate command groups, operation names,
project IDs, account names,

12
00:00:53.158 --> 00:00:59.389
resource names, regions, zones, file
paths, output paths, filters,

13
00:00:59.469 --> 00:01:02.262
and timeout values before use.

14
00:01:02.342 --> 00:01:07.928
Validation should favor expected formats,
approved scopes,

15
00:01:08.008 --> 00:01:15.313
and known target values, especially when
production resources may be affected.

16
00:01:15.393 --> 00:01:22.375
Working directory control matters when
scripts read templates, artifacts,

17
00:01:22.455 --> 00:01:28.041
policy files, deployment manifests, or
JSON configuration.

18
00:01:28.121 --> 00:01:33.600
A relative path should resolve from the
intended directory,

19
00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:37.547
not wherever the script happened to start.

20
00:01:37.627 --> 00:01:44.932
CLI version expectations also matter
because output formats, supported flags,

21
00:01:45.012 --> 00:01:51.672
and service behavior can vary across
installed versions and components.

22
00:01:51.752 --> 00:01:55.727
Safe stopping is part of secure
automation.

23
00:01:55.807 --> 00:01:58.922
Scripts should check exit status,

24
00:01:59.002 --> 00:02:03.729
distinguish expected empty results from
failures,

25
00:02:03.809 --> 00:02:08.643
and stop before later steps trust
incomplete output.

26
00:02:08.723 --> 00:02:16.135
Clear errors help operators recover, but
those errors should not expose secrets,

27
00:02:16.215 --> 00:02:21.908
raw tokens, service account key material,
sensitive IAM data,

28
00:02:21.988 --> 00:02:24.781
or excessive response bodies.

29
00:02:24.861 --> 00:02:30.340
A good wrapper is strict, understandable,
and easy to audit.
